1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,300
This is a real verdict Haruspec printed in August.

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,360
Equivalent.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:10,240
Sixty-four runs, sixty-four seam variants, and full coverage.

4
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:12,350
It meant almost nothing.

5
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:16,290
The spec had a mistyped seam key, so nothing ever read it.

6
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:21,670
All sixty-four variants were the same input, and the coverage was measured over a surface that

7
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:22,590
had quietly shrunk.

8
00:00:23,248 --> 00:00:25,208
A red that's wrong costs you an hour.

9
00:00:25,468 --> 00:00:28,648
A green that's wrong ships a bug through a gate you trusted.

10
00:00:29,028 --> 00:00:32,848
For an oracle, a false equivalent is the one catastrophic outcome.

11
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:35,308
So a verdict has to say what it saw.

12
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:42,533
Underneath, the diff can only say two things: the traces matched, or they didn't.

13
00:00:42,893 --> 00:00:46,133
Two layers above it turn that into one of four verdicts.

14
00:00:46,735 --> 00:00:47,515
Coverage first.

15
00:00:47,775 --> 00:00:49,335
It never hides a divergence.

16
00:00:49,755 --> 00:00:53,375
But an equivalent survives only when the coverage can stand behind it.

17
00:00:53,715 --> 00:00:55,935
Otherwise it becomes cannot verify.

18
00:00:56,685 --> 00:00:58,005
Then stability.

19
00:00:58,465 --> 00:01:03,445
If runs over identical inputs disagree, any verdict becomes unstable.

20
00:01:04,195 --> 00:01:08,295
Each verdict has an exit code, so one command is the gate.

21
00:01:08,595 --> 00:01:14,395
Zero for equivalent, three for diverged, four for cannot verify, five for unstable.

22
00:01:14,715 --> 00:01:17,395
And six for a declared change that never happened.

23
00:01:17,655 --> 00:01:19,375
Only zero is a pass.

24
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,960
That's why every verdict, green or red, carries a qualification block.

25
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:29,280
This one is real, from the engine's tests.

26
00:01:30,030 --> 00:01:36,890
Explored: almost four hundred runs, all seven orderings, each with fifty-five sets of seam values.

27
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:40,410
Stability: three replays, all identical.

28
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:43,810
Coverage: every declared boundary exercised.

29
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:48,490
Covered: the class and operations the claim is about, and where they're declared.

30
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:54,250
Seams: each seam that varied, how many readings it took, and how many boundary values ran.

31
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,400
And blind spots: what the capture couldn't see.

32
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,680
Not audited means nobody looked.

33
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:04,800
An empty list would be a claim: someone looked, and found nothing.

34
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,440
Below the block, a red says what changed.

35
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:15,580
Two divergences: the checkout event's total and checkout's return value, each one cent higher in the

36
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:15,900
candidate.

37
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:19,180
The port truncates a discount the old code rounded.

38
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:25,090
To reproduce it, the failing order: two adds, two promos, then checkout.

39
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:30,310
And the counterexample: the seam values that case read, a quantity of one,

40
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:30,870
twice.

41
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:36,100
A red keeps every failing case and reports the most informative one.

42
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:42,920
On a live campaign check, it also shrinks the order, dropping operations while the divergence holds.

43
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,640
A replay reports the order it recorded.

44
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:51,607
Cannot verify is the oracle declining to claim more than it saw.

45
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:57,307
Here's that mistake today, and the report names it: the declared domain matched nothing.

46
00:02:58,057 --> 00:03:00,357
Four gaps take a green away.

47
00:03:00,757 --> 00:03:05,497
A replay that fell back to a live read, for an input the recording never answered.

48
00:03:05,917 --> 00:03:07,797
A declared seam nothing read.

49
00:03:07,797 --> 00:03:13,977
An effect the source audit finds escaping capture, like a post to the global notification center.

50
00:03:14,777 --> 00:03:16,537
And source the audit couldn't read at all.

51
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:21,275
A boundary the run just never reached only lowers the percentage.

52
00:03:21,655 --> 00:03:24,335
Each gap is named, so you know where to look.

53
00:03:26,367 --> 00:03:29,807
Unstable means the same inputs gave different answers.

54
00:03:30,087 --> 00:03:34,627
A plan replays three times by default, and every pass must agree,

55
00:03:34,867 --> 00:03:36,907
down to each divergence's values.

56
00:03:37,657 --> 00:03:41,937
Here, runs one and three diverged, and run two matched.

57
00:03:42,337 --> 00:03:46,137
Without repetition, the gate would show whichever color it landed on.

58
00:03:46,697 --> 00:03:51,257
Instead, the report shows the delta between runs, and names the field that moved:

59
00:03:51,497 --> 00:03:53,557
a value the subject makes up for itself.

60
00:03:54,307 --> 00:03:59,187
On a red, a live campaign check reruns the baseline against itself instead.

61
00:03:59,627 --> 00:04:03,267
A fresh identifier or a clock reading usually turns up here.

62
00:04:03,667 --> 00:04:06,187
The seams video shows how to inject one.

63
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,020
Some differences aren't behavior.

64
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:16,160
A spec declares them once, in its comparison: numeric jitter, a timing budget,

65
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:22,960
two independent notifications in either order, a noisy channel to ignore, or comparison per concurrency domain.

66
00:04:23,710 --> 00:04:25,590
It applies at diff time.

67
00:04:25,930 --> 00:04:30,750
The recorded traces stay complete; the comparison only changes how they're read.

68
00:04:31,090 --> 00:04:34,130
And it can't excuse a value the subject makes up.

69
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:36,170
That's an uncontrolled seam.

70
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,460
Concurrency changes go the other way.

71
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:45,720
Work that moved to another domain, an ordering guarantee relaxed, or a synchronization the port dropped:

72
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:49,540
each gates by default, until you say you meant it.

73
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:53,393
Which is what intent is for.

74
00:04:53,733 --> 00:04:58,833
A branch doubles loyalty points, and the check goes red: sixty-three points before,

75
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:00,413
a hundred and twenty-six after.

76
00:05:00,773 --> 00:05:05,193
The report ends with the command that declares it, as narrow as the failing cases allow:

77
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:08,193
the points field, increasing, only in checkout.

78
00:05:08,943 --> 00:05:13,523
Run it, and the line lands in your branch's intent file, in the block a pull

79
00:05:13,523 --> 00:05:14,763
request description carries.

80
00:05:15,183 --> 00:05:18,783
The next check absorbs that change, and gates on whatever is left.

81
00:05:19,103 --> 00:05:21,483
Green, and it still shows what it absorbed.

82
00:05:21,823 --> 00:05:24,653
A declaration nothing matched fails with code six.

83
00:05:25,403 --> 00:05:28,583
That August green would read cannot verify today.

84
00:05:28,963 --> 00:05:32,843
A verdict proves what its block says it saw, and nothing more.
